From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] motor (4.4.94): no gpio direction change in the step interrupt

The TCU step interrupt (jz_timer_interrupt -> motor_move_step) drove the
phase pins with gpio_direction_output(). On the 4.4 Ingenic pinctrl that
ends in pinctrl_gpio_direction_output(), which takes pctldev->mutex, so
every step could sleep in hard-irq context: "BUG: scheduling while atomic"
(Cinnado D1 T41, PC in mgo), after which the move state stayed "running"
and rmmod hung.

Write only the level (gpio_set_value -> ingenic_gpio_set, PAT0 set/clear
register, no lock) from the step path. The pins become outputs once in
motor_set_default() (probe and the end of a reset, process context) via
the new mgo_dir(), which keeps gpio_direction_output().

The 3.10.14 motors-pp driver is not affected: there gpio_direction_output()
only takes a spinlock (gpiolib gpio_lock, soc gpio chip->gpio_lock).
---
--- a/common/misc/motor/motor.c
+++ b/common/misc/motor/motor.c
@@ -77,10 +77,26 @@
 		return !level;
 	return gpio_get_value(gpio);
 }
+/*
+ * Drive a phase pin from the TCU step interrupt.  Only the level register is
+ * written: on the 4.4 Ingenic pinctrl, gpio_direction_output() ends in
+ * pinctrl_gpio_direction_output(), which takes pctldev->mutex and must not be
+ * called in hard-irq context ("scheduling while atomic", then a stuck move
+ * state and an unkillable rmmod).  The pins are made outputs once by mgo_dir()
+ * in process context (probe, reset, stop).
+ */
 static void mgo(unsigned int gpio, int v)
 {
 	if (gpio == (unsigned int)-1)
 		return;
+	gpio_set_value(gpio, invert_gpio_dir ? !v : !!v);
+}
+/* configure a phase pin as output with a level; process context only (may sleep) */
+static void mgo_dir(unsigned int gpio, int v)
+{
+	might_sleep();
+	if (gpio == (unsigned int)-1)
+		return;
 	gpio_direction_output(gpio, invert_gpio_dir ? !v : !!v);
 }
 
@@ -121,10 +137,10 @@
 	for(index = 0; index < HAS_MOTOR_CNT; index++){
 		motor =  &mdev->motors[index];
 		motor->state = MOTOR_OPS_STOP;
-		mgo(motor->pdata->motor_st1_gpio, 0);
-		mgo(motor->pdata->motor_st2_gpio, 0);
-		mgo(motor->pdata->motor_st3_gpio, 0);
-		mgo(motor->pdata->motor_st4_gpio, 0);
+		mgo_dir(motor->pdata->motor_st1_gpio, 0);
+		mgo_dir(motor->pdata->motor_st2_gpio, 0);
+		mgo_dir(motor->pdata->motor_st3_gpio, 0);
+		mgo_dir(motor->pdata->motor_st4_gpio, 0);
 	}
 	return;
 }
